<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amir Kabir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mirza Taqi Khan Farahani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amir Kabir (Mirza Taqi Khan Farahani) was chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> under Naser al-Din Shah from 1848 to 1851. He reformed the finances and the army, curbed foreign influence and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-ul-funun">Dar ol-Fonun</a>, Iran's first modern college, which opened in 1851. Court rivals secured his dismissal, and he was put to death at Kashan in 1852; he is remembered in Iran as a pioneer of modernisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amir-kabir.jpg" alt="Amir Kabir" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painting: Muhammad Ibrahim Naghashbashi (? - 1851), Photo:Monfie in 2013 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmirKabir_naghashbashi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amir Kabir was born on 9 January 1807 in Hazaveh, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arak-iran">Arak</a> district. His father, Karbalaʾi Mohammad Qorban, was a cook in the service of Mirza Bozorg Qa'em-Maqam of Farahan, who saw signs of unusual talent in the boy and had him study with his own children. He entered government service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, rose to supervise the finances of the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and spent almost four years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> as part of a commission to delineate the Ottoman-Iranian frontier, which taught him the methods of international diplomacy. In 1848 he became chief tutor to the crown prince Naser al-Din, then fifteen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-shah-qajar">Mohammad Shah</a> died in September 1848, and Naser al-Din's minister could not raise the money for the journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, so Amir Kabir arranged it. The new shah made him head of the army and, in October 1848, chief minister with the titles amir-e kabir and atabak. The Queen Mother, Malek Jahan Khanom, and other princes resented the cuts he made in their allowances.</p><p>He tried to balance an empty treasury by cutting the salaries of civil servants, often by half, and by removing stipends of pensioners who did little work. He had Iranians vaccinated against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-ul-funun">Dar ol-Fonun</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, which taught medicine, mathematics and natural sciences, and whose Austrian instructors reached Tehran in November 1851, after his dismissal. He also founded the newspaper Vaqayeʿ-ye Ettefaqiyeh, sought to curb the power of the clergy, and followed a policy of granting concessions to neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>He regarded the Bábís as a threat. He suppressed the Babi uprisings of 1848 to 1851, personally ordered the public beheading of seven prominent Bábís in February 1850, and in mid-1850 ordered the execution of the Báb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. ʻAbdu'l-Bahá called him the worst oppressor of the religion but also acknowledged his reforms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Those whose incomes he had cut, among them the Queen Mother, formed a coalition against him with Mirza Aqa Khan Nuri and Mirza Yusuf Khan Ashtiyani, backed by foreign powers whose influence had diminished. Amir Kabir was demoted to head of the army, with Nuri made premier, and was then arrested and sent to Kashan. Six weeks later the Queen Mother and his executioner convinced the shah that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> would soon protect him, and he was murdered at Kashan on 10 January 1852. His services were generally unappreciated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> era, but modern Iranian historiography depicts him as one of the few capable and honest statesmen of the period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amir Kabir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Amir Kabir</b>, chief minister to Naser al-Din Shah in 1848–1851, reorganised finances, curbed abuses and founded the Dar ol-Fonun, a polytechnic college in Tehran, before he was dismissed and put to death in 1852.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hazaveh, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Kashan, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.18,49.53&amp;year=1836" aria-label="Hazaveh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5811 925 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amir Kabir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6261 1195h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6261 1195h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.18,49.53&amp;year=1836">What was here in 1836? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
